===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|magistrat|magistrat, maiestrat|t=magistrate; magistracy}}, borrowed from {{der|en|la|magistrātus}}. See also {{m|en|-ate|id=rank or office|pos=forms nouns denoting rank or office}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈmæd͡ʒɪstɹeɪt/|/ˈmæd͡ʒɪstɹət/}}
* {{audio|en|En-us-magistrate.ogg|a=US}}
* {{hyph|en|ma|gis|trate}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|legal}} A [[judicial]] [[officer]] with limited authority to administer and enforce the [[law]]. A magistrate's court may have jurisdiction in [[civil]] or [[criminal]] cases, or both.
# {{lb|en|historical}} A high official of the state or a municipality in ancient Greece or Rome.
# {{lb|en|by extension}} A comparable official in medieval or modern institutions.
#: {{ux|en|Like other civil servants, Ashok Kumar started his career as an Assistant Collector cum Sub-divisional '''Magistrate'''.}}
# {{lb|en|Quebec}} A [[master's degree]].

